Moment generating functions and independence Means and covariance matrices summarize a distribution. They do not determine it, and they cannot certify independence. For that we need a transform of the whole distribution. This section collects the facts that Chapter 3 and Chapter 4 use. [Moment generating function] The moment generating function of a p	imes1 random vector \Y is M_{\Y}( m t)=\E\exp( m t\T\Y),\qquad  m t\in\Real^p , where the expectation may be +\infty. We say that M_{\Y} exists if it is finite for all  m t in some neighbourhood \{
orm{ m t}<h\} of the origin, h>0. [Properties of moment generating functions] Suppose M_{\Y} exists. 1. Uniqueness. If M_{ m V} also exists and M_{\Y}=M_{ m V} on a neighbourhood of  zero, then \Y and  m V have the same distribution. 2. Moments. All moments of \Y are finite. The gradient of M_{\Y} at  zero is \E(\Y), and its Hessian at  zero is \E(\Y\Y\T). The Hessian of \log M_{\Y} at  zero is \Cov(\Y). 3. Affine maps. For a constant k	imes p matrix \A and k	imes1 vector  b, M_{\A\Y+ b}( m s)=\exp( m s\T b)\,M_{\Y}(\A\T m s). 4. Independence. Partition \Y=(\Y_1\T,\Y_2\T)\T and  m t=( m t_1\T, m t_2\T)\T conformably. Then \Y_1 and \Y_2 are independent iff M_{\Y}( m t)=M_{\Y_1}( m t_1)\,M_{\Y_2}( m t_2) for all  m t in a neighbourhood of  zero. For (a), the scalar case is a theorem of analysis: a moment generating function that is finite near the origin extends analytically, which pins down the characteristic function and hence the distribution (Billingsley 1995). For vectors, fix  m a. For small \lvert svert, M_{ m a\T\Y}(s)=M_{\Y}(s m a)=M_{ m V}(s m a)=M_{ m a\T m V}(s), so  m a\T\Y and  m a\T m V have the same distribution by the scalar case, and @prp-rv-cramer-wold below finishes the argument. For (b), finiteness near  zero implies \E\exp(h'\lvert Y_ivert)<\infty for some h'>0 and each i, so every polynomial in the components is integrable and differentiation under the expectation is justified. Then \partial M/\partial t_i=\E igl(Y_ie^{ m t\T\Y} igr) and \partial^2M/\partial t_i\partial t_j=\E igl(Y_iY_je^{ m t\T\Y} igr). At  m t= zero these are \E(Y_i) and \E(Y_iY_j). For K=\log M, the chain rule gives 
abla^2K=M^{-1}
